<style is:global>
  /* Global, because the rendered sections arrive through `set:html` and Astro's scoped
     styles do not reach markup this page did not write; every selector is prefixed with
     `data-security-page`, so nothing here can reach another page. Colours are W1.2's tokens
     and this file carries no colour literal.

     **The bold look, and where it comes from** (the owner's brief of 2026-10-21). The page is
     drawn in the same look the owner approved on the homepage and the product pages, and that
     look is `base.css`'s section 2c — one set of shapes for the whole site: the display scale,
     the gradient over a headline's words, the card and the panel tone. So this file names
     those classes rather than writing their rules again (a second copy of a shape is a shape
     that drifts from the homepage's), and the two things it adds are the page's own: the
     rhythm its four blocks are laid out in, and the panel the picture sits on. What it does
     **not** do is change a word: the page's text is the document's and the pointer's, and the
     look is a way of drawing them. `website/test/security.mjs`'s `boldLookFailures` is what
     holds the page to that — it reads the classes and the rhythm back off the built page —
     and the visual match to the owner's boards is the owner's own check at the preview
     (the redesign's 70a–70c, P9). */
  [data-security-page] {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    /* One column of blocks, set apart by one step of the spacing scale: the redesign's rhythm
       is the scale's, so the room between two of them is comparable with the room any other
       page gives its blocks, and the step is the page's own choice of how generous it is.

       **The gap is the only room between two blocks.** The four regions below carry no vertical
       margin of their own — the rule each one has says `margin: 0` — because a margin on a flex
       item is added to the gap, and a block that kept the `--space-lg` it carried while the page
       was a plain flow would give the gap twice: the room would render at 80 px where the page
       says one step. That is the defect `website/test/base-styles.mjs`'s `homeRhythmFailures`
       refuses on the homepage (a block padding a gap the block above it already pads), and the
       reading here is the same one: the room between two blocks is one step of the scale and
       not that step plus a block's own margin. `boldLookFailures` reads it back off this
       stylesheet, so the two rules cannot drift apart again. */
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-sm) var(--space-3xl);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The title band: the page's first screen, and the box the board's glow is drawn in.
     `position: relative` and `overflow: hidden` are what the glow layer needs, and the band's
     own top padding is the room above the headline — the article carries none, so the room is
     declared once. Prefixed with the page's marker like every other selector here, so the block
     cannot reach another page. */
  [data-security-page] .security-hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* The headline is `base.css`'s `.display-xl` with the one thing that is this page's: the
     width it is set to. `position: relative` is what paints it over the glow (a positioned box
     paints after a static one, and the glow is the band's first child), which is what the
     homepage's own hero does. */
  [data-security-page] .security-title {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  /* A section heading is the display scale's middle step — `base.css`'s `.display-md`, written
     on the element — so the hierarchy is the board's: the title at the top step and each
     section under it one step down. This rule is the room below the heading and nothing else,
     and it is written at `[data-security-page] h2` because a class and an attribute selector
     weigh the same: the page's own rule has to be the one that carries the margin, and
     `.display-md`'s `margin-block: 0` is what it is overriding. */
  [data-security-page] h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }

  /* The box the picture sits on is the panel and not the card's surface: the drawing's own
     cards are drawn on the surface, so a surface box around them would be a card on a card
     with nothing between them. One step below is what makes the picture read as a picture laid
     on the page, which is what the panel tone is for (`palette-logo.css`). The selector is the
     region's own attribute and the card's class together, so it beats `.card` whatever order
     the build emits the two rules in. */
  [data-security-diagram].card {
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  /* The pointer is the page's smallest block, so it is the card at the control's padding
     rather than at the card's: a full-height box under the reporting section would read as a
     fourth section rather than as the note it is. Its tone is the quieter one — it is a
     sentence about where the policy lives, not policy text.

     `margin: 0` is load-bearing here in a way it is not on the three sections: the pointer is a
     `<p>`, so `base.css`'s `:where(p) { margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm) }` gives it a margin below
     that the flex container's gap would be added to. The page does not cap this box either, for
     the reason the reporting card states — what holds this one sentence to the measure is the
     same site-wide `p` rule, and the page writing `max-width` here as well would be the page
     repeating a rule `base.css` already makes for every paragraph on the site. */
  [data-security-policy].card {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* The boundary diagram. **Two drawings, one picture, and exactly one of them rendered** (the
     owner's feedback of 2026-10-21: the tall drawing "works well in mobile" but "on desktop
     doesn't fit above the fold", so a desktop shows "a horizontal layout of the same drawing").
     The tall `<svg>` is the run read top to bottom in a 360-unit-wide `viewBox`, capped at
     22.5rem — a phone's own width, where a unit is a pixel — and it is the one a phone shows.
     The wide `<svg>` is the same run read left to right in a 1180 × 400 one, and it is the one
     a desktop shows: about 1056 px wide in the page's own column at 1440, which is 0.895 px a
     unit and about 358 px of picture, so the headline above it and the whole drawing sit inside
     a 1440 × 800 first screen (the arithmetic is in the drawing's own comment).

     The changeover is the width at which the wide drawing's labels stop being readable rather
     than the site's chrome breakpoint: its smallest label is 14 units and the narrowest column
     the page gives the drawing at 70rem is 1024 px — 72rem's own ceiling, an eighth of the
     site's gutter on each side and the card's `--space-lg` padding inside that — where 14 units
     are 12.15 px. Below 70rem the page shows the tall drawing, which is the whole picture at
     the scale a phone reads it at. The two are the same picture in the same order, so nothing
     is lost at the changeover: `website/test/security.mjs` reads both of them.

     **Only one is in the page at a time**, and `hidden` on the tall one is what a reader whose
     stylesheet never arrives gets: the wide drawing, which is the layout that carries the whole
     picture rather than a phone's cut of it. `display: none`/`block` in the query is what
     switches them, and the two rules are written last, after `.boundary-drawing`'s own
     `display: block`, because a class rule of the same weight as another is won by the one that
     comes later.

     The size is a `font-size` on each `<text>` and not a declaration here, because it is the
     drawing's own geometry: the gate measures a label against the box it is drawn in
     (`website/test/security.mjs`), and the box it has is a length in the picture's units, so
     the size it is drawn at has to be one too. 13 units is `--font-size-xs` (0.8125rem) and 20
     is `--font-size-lg` (1.25rem) at the 16 px root the tall picture's 360 units are laid out
     against, which is the width it is shown at; the wide drawing's own 14 and 21 are the same
     two sizes at the scale it is rendered at — the same arrangement, and the same estimate, as
     the How It Works drawing (`src/components/RunDiagram.astro`). No script reads or writes any
     of this; the shapes are painted from the classes below and every colour is a token. */
  [data-security-diagram] {
    margin: 0;
  }

  [data-security-diagram] figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* **The drawing is laid out in the column, and both numbers the page rests on are taken in that
     box**: `width: 100%` of the card's content box and no ceiling of the site's own
     (`max-width: 100%`), which is the box the label floor and the fold are measured in
     (`website/test/security.mjs`, and the pair the How It Works drawing is held to). A layout
     class may still cap its own drawing — the tall one does, at a phone's 22.5rem — and a cap
     shrinks the box the labels are read in, so the gate reads the cap rather than assuming the
     column. */
  [data-security-diagram] .boundary-drawing {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  [data-security-diagram] .boundary-drawing-tall {
    max-width: 22.5rem;
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 70rem) {
    [data-security-diagram] .boundary-drawing-wide {
      display: none;
    }

    [data-security-diagram] .boundary-drawing-tall {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  [data-security-diagram] text {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    fill: var(--text-muted);
  }

  [data-security-diagram] .boundary-title {
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--text);
  }

  [data-security-diagram] .boundary-heading {
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--accent);
  }

  [data-security-diagram] .boundary-card {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: var(--border-width);
